B-0 (Boosty) audit round 2, F9 -- O_APPEND means append: the open carries the
kernel's append omode bit instead of a one-time seek alone.

0030 emulated O_APPEND with one lseek(SEEK_END) at open, under a comment saying
"Thylacine has NO kernel append mode ... it needs ... a v1.x SYS_WALK_OPEN omode
bit". That bit has existed since VIVARIUM 6.27: OAPPEND (0x40), inside
SYS_WALK_OPEN_OMODE_VALID, which dev9p forwards to the 9P Tlopen / Tlcreate and
Stratum honours by positioning EVERY write at end-of-file server side. Raw
Linux-phenotype binaries (git) have had correct appends ever since; Pouch ports
were left on the emulation, and kernel/include/thylacine/syscall.h says so.

What the emulation got wrong: the seek happens once. fopen(p, "a+"), a read or
a rewind, then the write POSIX requires to land at END landed wherever the
cursor was -- mid-file, over existing bytes. Same for fseek + fwrite on an "a"
stream, and for two appenders to one log.

The fix passes the bit on all three open exits (create, EEXIST-fallback open,
plain open) and KEEPS the one-time seek: for an append fd the kernel cursor is
advisory, musl's __fdopen cannot set it (fcntl(F_SETFL) is ENOSYS here), and
the seek is what has made ftell() and the first read of an "a+" stream start
at end-of-file since 0030. A Dev with no append notion ignores the bit (each
switches on omode & 3). One difference from Linux remains and is stated in the
source: after an append write that followed a seek, the cursor is the seek
position plus the count, not the new end of file.

Cost: dev9p keeps an append fd off the Larder's write-behind path (pure
write-through is what is coherent for it), so each flushed append is one RPC.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-fopen
writes "AAAA", reopens "a+", seeks to 0, writes "BB", and requires the file to
read "AAAABB". On the 0030 emulation it reads "BBAA".

--- a/src/fcntl/openat.c
+++ b/src/fcntl/openat.c
@@ -29,20 +29,28 @@
 /* SYS_open +OTRUNC (SYS_WALK_OPEN_OMODE_VALID includes OTRUNC = 0x10). */
 #define POUCH_OTRUNC  0x10u
 
-/* O_APPEND (patch 0030). Thylacine has NO kernel append mode: an fd carries a
- * plain cursor, and musl's __fdopen tries fcntl(F_SETFL, O_APPEND) -- which
- * pouch answers ENOSYS -- so nothing positions the cursor. Without this seek
- * fopen("a") writes at offset 0 and CLOBBERS the file it meant to extend.
+/* O_APPEND, in two halves.
  *
- * Applied HERE, at a single helper every successful-open exit routes through,
- * rather than at one return site: openat() has three such exits (create-ok,
- * EEXIST-fallback-open, plain-open) and a per-site fix would silently miss
- * one. Error exits pass negatives through untouched.
+ * (1) The omode bit. The open syscalls carry an append bit (OAPPEND 0x40,
+ * inside SYS_WALK_OPEN_OMODE_VALID since VIVARIUM 6.27) that dev9p forwards to
+ * the 9P open, and Stratum then positions EVERY write at end-of-file server
+ * side -- the POSIX meaning. Patch 0030 predates the bit and only seeked once
+ * (half 2), so fopen("a+"), a read or an fseek, then a write landed MID-FILE.
+ * A Dev without an append notion ignores the bit (it switches on omode & 3).
  *
- * Single-writer append is thereby correct (the config-file / log-file case
- * fopen("a") is actually used for). CONCURRENT appenders may interleave --
- * that atomicity is documented-ABSENT, not silently claimed; it needs a real
- * kernel append mode (a v1.x SYS_WALK_OPEN omode bit). */
+ * (2) The one-time seek, kept: for an append fd the kernel cursor is advisory,
+ * and musl's __fdopen cannot set it (its fcntl(F_SETFL) is ENOSYS here), so
+ * this is what makes ftell() and the first read of an "a+" stream start at
+ * end-of-file, as they have since 0030. After an append write that followed a
+ * seek the cursor is the seek position plus the count, not the new end of file
+ * as on Linux; a stream must reposition between a write and a read anyway.
+ *
+ * Both are applied where every open exit routes through -- the omode before
+ * the three opens, the seek in one helper -- rather than per return site:
+ * openat() has three successful exits (create-ok, EEXIST-fallback-open,
+ * plain-open) and a per-site fix would silently miss one. */
+#define POUCH_OAPPEND 0x40u
+
 static int pouch_open_ret(long r, int flags)
 {
 	if (r >= 0 && (flags & O_APPEND))
@@ -74,6 +82,7 @@
 	case O_RDWR:   omode = POUCH_ORDWR;  break;
 	default:       errno = EINVAL; return -1;
 	}
+	if (flags & O_APPEND) omode |= POUCH_OAPPEND;
 
 	size_t len = strnlen(filename, POUCH_PATH_MAX + 1);
 	if (!len) { errno = ENOENT; return -1; }
